course: dictionary
lesson: 3675
title: Fizz: a word that bubbles
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one job and more than one meaning. Fizz can be a doing word (a verb) or a thing (a noun). «The drink began to fizz.» Here fizz is what the drink does: it makes tiny bubbles with a hissing sound.
explain: Context shows you which meaning is right. «The room fizzed with excitement.» There are no bubbles in a room, so fizzed must mean full of energy and excitement. Ask yourself: does the bubble meaning make sense here? If not, try another meaning.
explain: Fizz also has a phrase: «fizz out» means to end weakly, with no big finish. Phrases like this are often not clear from single words, so look at the whole group of words and at the sentence around it.
fact: Fizz can be a verb (fizz, fizzes, fizzed, fizzing) or a noun (fizz).
fact: «Fizz» means tiny gas bubbles with a hissing sound, like a fizzy drink.
fact: In science, vinegar and bicarbonate of soda fizz because they make carbon dioxide gas.
fact: «Fizzy drinks lose their fizz when left open.» A tight lid keeps the fizz best.
fact: In UK informal speech, a fizz can also mean a fizzy drink, and informally it can mean excitement or energy.
exercise: «The room fizzed with excitement.» What does fizzed mean here? | Went flat | Made tiny bubbles | Hissed like a snake | *Full of excitement and energy
exercise: In a science test, vinegar and bicarbonate of soda make a fizz. What is the fizz? | A type of metal | *Tiny gas bubbles with a hissing sound | A cold, hard rock | A sad feeling
exercise: Sherbet «fizzes in your mouth and not in the packet». Why? | The packet is too cold | *It only reacts when it gets wet | It has no acid in it | It is always dry and sealed in a tin
test: «The party fizzed all evening.» What does fizzed mean here? | Went flat and quiet | Made tiny bubbles in the cups | Was cancelled | *Was full of energy and fun
test: Which phrase means to end weakly? | *Fizz out | Fizz up | Fizz away fast | Fizz down
exam: Which sentence uses fizz as a verb (a doing word)? | Fizz is my favourite word | *The soda started to fizz as I opened it | The fizz was gone by morning | There was a fizz in the glass
